      "passage": "He was called Raj and he came from Kolkata where he lived with his father in a small dark room with no windows. Now he lived in a bright and clean boarding house in a dingy area of Portsmouth in England which was to him an improvement compared to what he had growing up.\n\nRaj had been a cleaner on a cruise liner and part of a team responsible for cleaning cabins. He took great pride in his work, often going above and beyond what was asked of him. He would put little chocolates in the rooms he took care of and help his co-workers by doing extra shifts when asked. Thus, he was often singled out for compliments by both staff and passengers. On his last voyage on the ship, he developed appendicitis and was put ashore in England. The operation had gone uneventfully but the wound became infected and Raj spent two weeks poised between life and death. By the time he was discharged from hospital, his ship had sailed on to Norway. The shipping company managed to arrange permission for him to stay in the country and suggested he work on land for a while until he recovered.\n\nHe then applied to a local employment agency. He was interviewed by a man and after ten minutes, he was offered the job of looking after the toilets at a railway station. He accepted it.\n\nHe sent a letter to his father who could not read or write and would have it read to him by his neighbour. \"I have a very good job now. The pay is good and the duties are light,\" he wrote. The neighbour wrote back to tell him that his father was very proud of him.\n\nRaj was a hard worker and he found there was much to do at the station toilets. They had not been decorated for years and Raj decided to do something about that himself. He bought tins of paint, a folding ladder and the necessary brushes and rollers. He completed the redecoration over a weekend, working late into the night.\n\nThe station manager came to see him, asking if he had done this himself. Raj nodded. He was concerned he might get into trouble for acting without permission but the station manager merely smiled and thanked him, saying he had done a fine job.\n\nMembers of the public began to take note, complimenting Raj on the gleaming taps, the spotless, well-scrubbed floor and the scented flowers that he put out at his own expense. The local newspaper sent a reporter to write a full-page article on the exemplary toilets after hearing about them from many of their readers. Raj sent a copy of it to his father, who wrote back, through the neighbour, to say, \"I am the proudest father.\"\n\nOne morning, a policeman came into the toilet and waited by the door. Raj wondered whether he was planning to arrest somebody. However, another man came in. This was the prime minister. The prime minister said to Raj, \"I read about your work in the paper. Imagine if everybody in this country took this sort of pride in their work.\" Raj beamed with pleasure.\n\nThen the prime minister asked if he needed anything. Raj thought. He bit his lip. Then he said, \"My father, sir. He is on his own in India and I would like to have him here so that I can take care of him.\"\n\nThe sun came in through a high window and shone on the highly polished taps. The prime minister hesitated. Then, he said, \"Sometimes, I can help.\" Then he added, \"Sometimes.\"\n\nRaj looked down at the floor and squeezed his eyes shut as he realised the audacity of his question. Had he crossed some line of which he was unaware? Another man in a suit soon appeared. The prime minister whispered something to him. The man nodded, looked at Raj, and smiled.",
